WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
GridGuide 1.1.0 release candidate. Independently authored fictional examples, not live utility observations. No deployment or safety certification.

2
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Declared power paths distinguish repair prerequisites from customer dependencies. Python prepared these milestone results.

3
00:00:45.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Completed means the fictional dependency repairs are complete at this modeled milestone. It does not mean utility-confirmed restoration.

4
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Blocked access has unknown clearance. Full modeled completion stays unavailable; it is never converted to a finite ETR.

5
00:01:36.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Unresolved evidence stays visible. No validated outage probability or empirically calibrated restoration confidence is available.

6
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Four policies use identical assumptions. Critical-first improves one critical latency but worsens total customer-hours. Release/person-hour remains the default; optional CP-SAT is frozen and experimental.

7
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:51.000
This supported precedence override is recomputed by Python and independently validated. Changing the scenario or review invalidates earlier acknowledgement.

8
00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Explicit acknowledgements produce a reproducible fictional review record. Checksums establish consistency, not authenticated reviewer identity or tamper-proof history.

9
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Actual CLI output from the downloaded JSON. Replay recomputes baselines and validates metrics; it does not rerun CP-SAT.

10
00:03:37.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Actual server shutdown and browser networking disabled. Only the reviewed kit and explicitly saved historical fiction remain. No browser numerical engine or live outage awareness.
